---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { loadContent } from "../../lib/content";
import { calBookingUrl } from "../../lib/site";

const { provider } = loadContent();
---

<Layout
  title={`Globalisation and the Indian Economy | CBSE Class 10 Economics | ${provider.name}`}
  description="CBSE Class 10 Economics Chapter 4 study notes on Globalisation and the Indian Economy. MNCs, foreign trade and market integration, factors enabling globalisation (technology & liberalisation), WTO, impact on India, SEZs, and the struggle for fair globalisation with 20 board exam quiz questions."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="small" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> / <a href="/subjects/">Subjects</a> / <a href="/resources/">Resources</a> / Globalisation and the Indian Economy
    </nav>

    <div class="resource-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">CBSE Class 10 · Social Science · Economics Chapter 4</span>
      <h1>Globalisation and the Indian Economy</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Trace the integration of nations: the rise of Multinational Corporations (MNCs), containerization and digital connectivity, trade barriers and the 1991 liberalization, WTO rules, Special Economic Zones, and the movement towards fair globalization.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- Core Concepts -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Foundational Globalisation Concepts</h2>

      <div class="grid two concept-grid">
        <article class="card">
          <h3>🌐 MNCs: Production Across Countries</h3>
          <p class="formula">Multinational Corporations (MNCs)</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Definition:</strong> An MNC is a company that owns or controls production in more than one nation. Production is organized in increasingly complex ways to minimize cost and maximize profit (e.g. designed in the US, manufactured in China, call-center customer care in India).</li>
            <li><strong>How MNCs Interlink Production:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Direct Investment (FDI):</em> Buying land, buildings, and machinery in foreign markets.
              <br/>• <em>Joint Ventures:</em> Partnering with local firms (e.g. Ford Motors with Mahindra).
              <br/>• <em>Acquiring Local Companies:</em> Cargill Foods (American MNC) buying Parakh Foods in India.
              <br/>• <em>Placing Orders with Small Producers:</em> Garments, footwear, and sports items produced by thousands of small suppliers sold under MNC brand names (Nike, Zara).
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🚢 Foreign Trade &amp; Integration of Markets</h3>
          <p class="formula">Movement of Goods, Services, Capital &amp; Labour</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Connecting Domestic Markets:</strong> Foreign trade gives producers opportunity to reach beyond domestic frontiers. For buyers, import expands choice of goods beyond what is domestically produced (e.g. Chinese toys in Indian markets).</li>
            <li><strong>Price Equalisation:</strong> Prices of similar goods in two markets tend to become equal; producers in both nations compete closely despite geographic distance.</li>
            <li><strong>Definition of Globalisation:</strong> Globalisation is the process of rapid integration or interconnection between countries through trade, investment, technology, and migration of people.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>⚡ Factors Enabling Globalisation &amp; Liberalisation</h3>
          <p class="formula">Technology &amp; Policy Shift in 1991</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>Rapid Improvement in Technology:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Transportation:</em> Containerisation dramatically cut port turnaround times and freight costs across oceans.
              <br/>• <em>Information &amp; Communication Technology (IT):</em> Internet, email, satellite communication, and smartphones enable instant global coordination and offshoring of back-office services.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Liberalisation of Foreign Trade &amp; Investment (1991):</strong>
              <br/>• After independence, India put <strong>trade barriers</strong> (import tariffs and quotas) to protect infant domestic industries from foreign competition.
              <br/>• In 1991, India adopted the New Economic Policy, removing barriers to allow Indian producers to compete with global manufacturers and attract modern technology and foreign capital.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <article class="card">
          <h3>🏛️ WTO, Impact on India &amp; Fair Globalisation</h3>
          <p class="formula">World Trade Organization &amp; Societal Distribution</p>
          <ul class="clean-list">
            <li><strong>World Trade Organization (WTO):</strong> International body aiming to liberalize international trade. Nearly 164 member nations. Rules often favor developed countries (e.g. US farmers receive massive subsidies while developing countries are pressured to stop agricultural subsidies).</li>
            <li><strong>Impact on India:</strong>
              <br/>• <em>Winners:</em> Well-off consumers (greater choices, higher quality, lower prices), top Indian MNCs (Tata Motors, Infosys, Ranbaxy), and IT service providers.
              <br/>• <em>Strugglers:</em> Small manufacturers (batteries, plastics, toys, dairy) hit by cheaper imports; workers facing insecure, flexible contractual employment.
            </li>
            <li><strong>Special Economic Zones (SEZs):</strong> Industrial zones with world-class infrastructure, tax holidays for 5 years, and flexible labour laws to attract MNC investment.</li>
            <li><strong>The Struggle for Fair Globalisation:</strong> Government policies must protect workers' rights, support small producers until strong, negotiate balanced WTO rules, and align with developing nations.</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Visual Diagrams Section -->
    <section class="section">
      <h2>Visual Concepts: MNC Production Network &amp; The Two Sides of Globalisation</h2>

      <div class="grid two">
        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 1 · The Global Production Web of an MNC</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="MNC Global Supply Chain">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Center: MNC HQ -->
              <circle cx="230" cy="125" r="45" fill="#1e293b" stroke="#0f172a" stroke-width="2"/>
              <text x="230" y="120" fill="#ffffff" font-size="11" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">MNC HQ</text>
              <text x="230" y="135" fill="#94a3b8" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">USA / Europe</text>

              <!-- Node 1: R&D (Top Left) -->
              <rect x="30" y="30" width="125" height="50" fill="#eff6ff" stroke="#3b82f6" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="92" y="50" fill="#1d4ed8" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">R&amp;D &amp; Design</text>
              <text x="92" y="66" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">USA / Japan (Tech)</text>

              <!-- Node 2: Manufacturing (Top Right) -->
              <rect x="305" y="30" width="125" height="50" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="367" y="50" fill="#991b1b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Manufacturing</text>
              <text x="367" y="66" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">China / Vietnam (Low cost)</text>

              <!-- Node 3: Customer Care (Bottom Left) -->
              <rect x="30" y="170" width="125" height="50" fill="#f0fdf4" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="92" y="190" fill="#15803d" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Customer Service</text>
              <text x="92" y="206" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">India (Fluent English &amp; IT)</text>

              <!-- Node 4: Assembly & Markets (Bottom Right) -->
              <rect x="305" y="170" width="125" height="50" fill="#fef3c7" stroke="#d97706" stroke-width="1.5" rx="6"/>
              <text x="367" y="190" fill="#b45309" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Assembly &amp; Sales</text>
              <text x="367" y="206" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Mexico / E. Europe (Near market)</text>

              <!-- Connecting Lines -->
              <line x1="155" y1="55" x2="195" y2="95" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
              <line x1="305" y1="55" x2="265" y2="95" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
              <line x1="155" y1="195" x2="195" y2="155" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
              <line x1="305" y1="195" x2="265" y2="155" stroke="#64748b" stroke-width="2"/>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">MNCs slice production into geographical fragments to exploit competitive cost advantages across the globe.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card visual-card">
          <div class="visual-badge">Visual Diagram 2 · The Two Faces of Globalisation in India</div>
          <div class="svg-container">
            <svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-label="Two sides of Globalisation">
              <rect width="460" height="250" fill="#f8fafc" rx="8"/>
              
              <!-- Positive Impact -->
              <rect x="30" y="35" width="185" height="185" fill="#f0fdf4" stroke="#16a34a" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/>
              <text x="122" y="62" fill="#15803d" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">BENEFICIARIES</text>
              <line x1="45" y1="72" x2="200" y2="72" stroke="#86efac" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="122" y="95" fill="#1e293b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">• Urban Well-off Consumers</text>
              <text x="122" y="112" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Cheaper cars, electronics, phones</text>
              <text x="122" y="135" fill="#1e293b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">• Indian MNCs &amp; IT Giants</text>
              <text x="122" y="152" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Tata Motors, Infosys, Ranbaxy</text>
              <text x="122" y="175" fill="#1e293b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">• High-tech skilled labor</text>
              <text x="122" y="195" fill="#15803d" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Higher wages &amp; global mobility</text>

              <!-- Negative Impact -->
              <rect x="245" y="35" width="185" height="185" fill="#fef2f2" stroke="#ef4444" stroke-width="2" rx="8"/>
              <text x="337" y="62" fill="#991b1b" font-size="12" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">VULNERABLE GROUPS</text>
              <line x1="260" y1="72" x2="415" y2="72" stroke="#fca5a5" stroke-width="1.5"/>
              <text x="337" y="95" fill="#1e293b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">• Small-Scale Producers</text>
              <text x="337" y="112" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Batteries, plastic, toy factories shut</text>
              <text x="337" y="135" fill="#1e293b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">• Unorganized Workers</text>
              <text x="337" y="152" fill="#475569" font-size="8.5" text-anchor="middle">Flexible contracts, no job security</text>
              <text x="337" y="175" fill="#1e293b" font-size="10" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">• Traditional Craftsmen</text>
              <text x="337" y="195" fill="#b91c1c" font-size="9" font-weight="bold" text-anchor="middle">Undercut by cheap mass imports</text>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <p class="caption">The dual nature of globalization creates vast prosperity for skilled elites, but poses severe displacement risks for small enterprises.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Board Exam Quiz Section (20 Questions) -->
    <section class="section">
      <div class="quiz-header">
        <div>
          <h2>CBSE Board Examination Quiz</h2>
          <p class="lead" style="margin-bottom:0;">Test your understanding of MNCs, Trade Liberalisation, WTO, SEZs, and Fair Globalisation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="quiz-score-badge" id="quizScoreBadge">Score: 0 / 20</div>
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-container" id="quizContainer">
        <!-- JS rendered -->
      </div>

      <div class="quiz-footer-actions">
        <button type="button" class="button" id="resetQuizBtn">Reset Quiz</button>
        <span class="small" id="quizProgressText">0 of 20 answered</span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Call to Action Banner -->
    <section class="cta-banner">
      <h2>Excel in CBSE Class 10 Economics</h2>
      <p>Master trade barrier analysis, SEZs, multinational case studies, and assertion-reasoning questions with expert guidance.</p>
      <a href={calBookingUrl} class="button">Book a Doubt Clearing Session</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script is:inline>
  const questions = [
    {
      q: "1. What is a Multinational Corporation (MNC)?",
      options: [
        "A company that owns or controls production of goods and services in more than one nation",
        "A firm that operates exclusively inside municipal city limits",
        "A charity organization funded by the United Nations",
        "A state-owned corporation with zero private employees"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "An MNC is an enterprise that owns or controls production facilities in multiple countries, seeking cost efficiencies and global markets."
    },
    {
      q: "2. Why do MNCs set up production units in developing countries like China, India, and Vietnam?",
      options: [
        "Because these countries have cheap labour, skilled technical manpower, and proximity to large markets",
        "Because taxes are completely prohibited in these countries",
        "Because raw materials are provided for free by international banks",
        "Because environmental laws are non-existent worldwide"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "MNCs invest in developing nations because lower wage costs, skilled engineers, and vast consumer markets help reduce production costs by 50% to 60%."
    },
    {
      q: "3. What is an investment made by an MNC to acquire assets (like factories, machinery, and land) in a foreign country called?",
      options: [
        "Domestic Capital Expenditure",
        "Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)",
        "Public Provident Fund",
        "Municipal Bond"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Money that is spent by foreign companies to buy assets such as land, building, and machinery abroad is called Foreign Direct Investment (FDI)."
    },
    {
      q: "4. Which of the following is a common way in which MNCs control production in other countries?",
      options: [
        "Buying up local companies and expanding their production",
        "Entering into joint ventures with domestic companies",
        "Placing orders with small domestic producers under the MNC brand name",
        "All of the above"
      ],
      correct: 3,
      explanation: "MNCs enter markets through multiple routes: direct acquisitions (e.g. Cargill buying Parakh Foods), joint ventures (Ford with Mahindra), and contract manufacturing."
    },
    {
      q: "5. What is the fundamental outcome of foreign trade on domestic markets?",
      options: [
        "It limits buyers to locally produced items only",
        "It connects markets in different countries, expands consumer choices, and equalizes prices across borders",
        "It forces all domestic firms to declare bankruptcy",
        "It bans the use of container ships"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Foreign trade creates an opportunity for producers to reach overseas buyers and gives consumers choices beyond goods produced locally."
    },
    {
      q: "6. What is 'Globalisation' defined as?",
      options: [
        "The process of rapid integration or interconnection among countries through trade, foreign investment, and technology",
        "The isolation of a nation's culture from foreign influence",
        "The migration of all citizens from villages to big cities",
        "The establishment of a single global language"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Globalisation is the process of rapid integration or interconnection among countries, characterized by cross-border movement of goods, services, capital, and people."
    },
    {
      q: "7. Which technological innovation revolutionised the transportation of goods across the oceans at drastically lower costs?",
      options: [
        "Steam locomotives",
        "Containerisation (cargo loaded into standardized containers directly onto ships, trains, and trucks)",
        "Hot air balloons",
        "Horse carriages"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Containers have led to huge reductions in port handling costs and increased the speed with which exports reach global markets."
    },
    {
      q: "8. What is a 'Trade Barrier'?",
      options: [
        "Restrictions (such as taxes on imports or quotas) imposed by a government to regulate the volume and type of goods entering a country",
        "A concrete wall constructed along sea ports",
        "A physical gate at airport customs",
        "A legal ban on printing currencies"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Trade barriers are regulatory mechanisms like tariffs and quotas used by governments to protect domestic producers from foreign competition."
    },
    {
      q: "9. Why did the Indian government put barriers to foreign trade and foreign investment after independence in 1947?",
      options: [
        "To protect emerging domestic producers from intense competition from established foreign manufacturers",
        "Because India had no ships to trade with foreign countries",
        "Because the British government ordered India not to trade",
        "Because foreign trade was prohibited by religious scriptures"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Just after independence, Indian industries were at an infant stage; barriers protected domestic manufacturing until industries achieved competitiveness."
    },
    {
      q: "10. In which year did India initiate major economic reforms to liberalise foreign trade and investment?",
      options: [
        "1947",
        "1971",
        "1991",
        "2014"
      ],
      correct: 2,
      explanation: "In 1991, India adopted the New Economic Policy (NEP), dismantling import quotas, cutting tariffs, and welcoming foreign direct investment."
    },
    {
      q: "11. What does 'Liberalisation' mean in the context of economic policy?",
      options: [
        "Removing barriers or restrictions set by the government on trade and business",
        "Making all education completely free in private colleges",
        "Abolishing all courts of law",
        "Requiring all companies to be owned by the army"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Liberalisation refers to removing state-imposed restrictions, bureaucratic permits, and barriers on foreign trade and investment."
    },
    {
      q: "12. What is the stated primary objective of the World Trade Organization (WTO)?",
      options: [
        "To liberalise international trade and promote free, rules-based commerce among nations",
        "To collect taxes for the United Nations military",
        "To fix the prices of all consumer goods worldwide",
        "To run international airlines"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "The WTO aims to liberalise international trade by establishing multilateral rules and negotiating the removal of trade barriers."
    },
    {
      q: "13. What is a major criticism leveled against the World Trade Organization by developing nations?",
      options: [
        "It forces developing nations to remove barriers while developed countries like the US continue to heavily subsidize their own agricultural exports",
        "It holds all meetings in developing countries",
        "It bans developing countries from joining",
        "It provides loans only to wealthy nations"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Developing countries argue that WTO rules are unfair: developed nations retain massive agricultural subsidies while forcing developing nations to open their markets."
    },
    {
      q: "14. Which Indian company is an example of an emerging Multinational Corporation (MNC)?",
      options: [
        "Tata Motors (automobiles)",
        "Infosys (IT services)",
        "Ranbaxy (pharmaceuticals)",
        "All of the above"
      ],
      correct: 3,
      explanation: "Indian companies such as Tata Motors, Infosys, Ranbaxy, Asian Paints, and Sundaram Fasteners have expanded operations globally as MNCs."
    },
    {
      q: "15. What are 'Special Economic Zones' (SEZs)?",
      options: [
        "Designated industrial zones with world-class facilities (electricity, water, roads) and tax holidays to attract foreign investment",
        "Agricultural lands where farming is prohibited by international decree",
        "Border areas reserved exclusively for military drills",
        "Forest reserves where no human entry is permitted"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "SEZs are industrial areas developed by governments offering top-notch infrastructure, 5-year tax exemptions, and flexible labor laws to attract MNC investment."
    },
    {
      q: "16. Which domestic sector in India was severely hit by competition from cheap imports under globalisation?",
      options: [
        "Information Technology",
        "Small-scale manufacturing (batteries, capacitors, plastic toys, dairy, vegetable oil)",
        "Call center customer support",
        "Software programming"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Small-scale manufacturing industries employing millions of workers suffered shutdowns and job losses due to inexpensive mass-produced imports (e.g. from China)."
    },
    {
      q: "17. How has globalisation impacted employment conditions for industrial workers in India?",
      options: [
        "It has made all jobs permanent with lifetime pensions",
        "Workers are increasingly hired on a flexible, temporary, contractual basis to keep labor costs low for global competitiveness",
        "It has doubled the paid vacation time for factory workers",
        "It has banned overtime work completely"
      ],
      correct: 1,
      explanation: "Intense global competition has forced employers to adopt 'flexible labor', hiring workers on temporary contracts with no job security or benefits."
    },
    {
      q: "18. What does 'Fair Globalisation' demand?",
      options: [
        "That globalisation creates opportunities for all and benefits are shared better among all strata of society",
        "That all foreign trade is banned permanently",
        "That rich countries receive 100% of global profits",
        "That developing nations stop using modern computers"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Fair globalisation emphasizes that trade policies should benefit small producers, protect workers' statutory rights, and ensure equitable prosperity."
    },
    {
      q: "19. How can governments ensure that globalisation becomes fairer?",
      options: [
        "Strictly enforcing labour laws to ensure workers get their rights",
        "Supporting small producers to improve their performance until they are strong enough to compete",
        "Negotiating at the WTO for fairer rules in alliance with other developing nations",
        "All of the above"
      ],
      correct: 3,
      explanation: "Governments can play a crucial role by enforcing labor standards, shielding vulnerable small manufacturers, and forming coalitions to demand parity at the WTO."
    },
    {
      q: "20. Why do consumers from urban well-off sections enjoy greater benefits from globalisation?",
      options: [
        "They have greater choice of higher-quality goods (automobiles, electronics, clothing) at competitive, lower prices",
        "They are exempted from paying income tax by MNCs",
        "MNCs provide them with free housing",
        "They are given diplomatic passports by the WTO"
      ],
      correct: 0,
      explanation: "Urban affluent consumers have benefited significantly from enhanced choices, improved product quality, and competitive pricing across consumer durables."
    }
  ];

  function renderQuiz() {
    const container = document.getElementById('quizContainer');
    if (!container) return;
    container.innerHTML = '';

    questions.forEach((item, idx) => {
      const qCard = document.createElement('div');
      qCard.className = 'quiz-card';
      qCard.id = `qCard_${idx}`;

      const title = document.createElement('h3');
      title.className = 'quiz-question';
      title.textContent = item.q;
      qCard.appendChild(title);

      const optList = document.createElement('div');
      optList.className = 'quiz-options';

      item.options.forEach((optText, optIdx) => {
        const btn = document.createElement('button');
        btn.type = 'button';
        btn.className = 'quiz-opt-btn';
        btn.textContent = optText;
        btn.addEventListener('click', () => handleSelectOption(idx, optIdx));
        optList.appendChild(btn);
      });
      qCard.appendChild(optList);

      const feedback = document.createElement('div');
      feedback.className = 'quiz-feedback';
      feedback.id = `feedback_${idx}`;
      qCard.appendChild(feedback);

      container.appendChild(qCard);
    });
  }

  let userAnswers = {};

  function handleSelectOption(qIdx, optIdx) {
    if (userAnswers[qIdx] !== undefined) return;
    userAnswers[qIdx] = optIdx;

    const q = questions[qIdx];
    const isCorrect = (optIdx === q.correct);
    const card = document.getElementById(`qCard_${qIdx}`);
    const btns = card.querySelectorAll('.quiz-opt-btn');
    const fb = document.getElementById(`feedback_${qIdx}`);

    btns.forEach((btn, i) => {
      btn.disabled = true;
      if (i === q.correct) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-correct');
      } else if (i === optIdx) {
        btn.classList.add('opt-wrong');
      }
    });

    if (isCorrect) {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-correct';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Correct!</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    } else {
      fb.className = 'quiz-feedback feedback-wrong';
      fb.innerHTML = `<strong>Incorrect.</strong> ${q.explanation}`;
    }

    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  function updateScoreSummary() {
    let score = 0;
    const answeredCount = Object.keys(userAnswers).length;
    for (let qIdx in userAnswers) {
      if (userAnswers[qIdx] === questions[qIdx].correct) {
        score++;
      }
    }
    const badge = document.getElementById('quizScoreBadge');
    if (badge) badge.textContent = `Score: ${score} / ${questions.length}`;

    const prog = document.getElementById('quizProgressText');
    if (prog) prog.textContent = `${answeredCount} of ${questions.length} answered`;
  }

  function resetQuiz() {
    userAnswers = {};
    renderQuiz();
    updateScoreSummary();
  }

  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    renderQuiz();
    const resetBtn = document.getElementById('resetQuizBtn');
    if (resetBtn) resetBtn.addEventListener('click', resetQuiz);
  });
</script>

<style>
  .wrap {
    max-width: 960px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem;
  }

  nav.small {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  nav.small a {
    color: var(--brand);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .resource-header {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .eyebrow {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.05em;
    color: var(--brand);
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .section {
    margin-bottom: 3.5rem;
  }

  .section h2 {
    font-size: 1.6rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.5rem;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);
  }

  .grid {
    display: grid;
